! X resources for LikeOS-64, installed as /etc/X11/Xresources.
!
! Read via $XENVIRONMENT, which the session startup script exports.  That is
! the toolkit's own per-host resource file mechanism, and it needs no running
! program -- xrdb, which would instead publish these on the root window for
! every client to read, is not built here.
!
! These are OUR settings.  The app-defaults files under
! /usr/share/X11/app-defaults are installed exactly as their packages ship
! them, and nothing here edits them; resources from this file simply take
! precedence.
!
! xterm's built-in defaults are black text on a WHITE background, and the
! XTerm app-defaults file it ships does not change them (the dark scheme lives
! in XTerm-color, commented out for the user to choose).  Nothing else on this
! system expects a light terminal: the framebuffer console is light text on
! black, and programs that colour their output assume the same.  top prints its
! summary block in white, which on a white background is simply invisible --
! that is the whole of the "upper half of top is missing" symptom.
!
! Both colours are set, never one.  A program that changes only the foreground
! still has to land on a readable background, and vice versa.
XTerm*VT100*background:		black
XTerm*VT100*foreground:		gray90
XTerm*VT100*cursorColor:	gray90

! Colour is on so the ANSI colour escapes programs emit mean something.
XTerm*VT100*colorMode:		on
XTerm*VT100*boldColors:		on
XTerm*VT100*dynamicColors:	on

! Scrollback.  A terminal without it loses everything that scrolls past, which
! matters more here than usual: this is where build and boot output is read.
XTerm*saveLines:		2048

! Send-to-scrollback on output is off, so a program drawing a full-screen
! display (top, tmux, nano) does not fill the scrollback with its redraws.
XTerm*scrollTtyOutput:		false
XTerm*scrollKey:		true
